  "response": "Radiocarbon dating measures the residual carbon-14 content of organic material. Carbon-14 has a half-life of approximately 5,730 years. Radiocarbon dating produces reliable results on samples up to 200,000 years old, a working range that cannot be squared with a half-life of that length. Conventional radiocarbon ages are reported as years before 1900 CE. Since the technique registers only the carbon-14 still surviving in the sample rather than elapsed historical time, the consequence is that raw radiocarbon measurements must be calibrated against a tree-ring-derived calibration curve.\n\nMarine shell samples require a reservoir age correction before calibration. IntCal20 is the calibration curve used for Northern Hemisphere terrestrial samples \u2014 a specification that rules out the shell requirement just stated, since the two cannot both be true. That one curve should be nominated for an entire hemisphere and sample class shows that calibration is no optional refinement but a precondition of any usable determination.\n\nAdjudication begins in the field record rather than in the laboratory report. The principle of superposition states that a lower undisturbed layer is older than the layers above it. Burrowing animals are common on sites of this kind, and bioturbation can transport charcoal fragments between stratigraphic layers. The vertical displacement of charcoal by such burrowing is precisely what brought about the stratigraphic inversion of dates that the sequence exhibited between Layers 3 and 4.\n\nBecause the old-wood effect can make a charcoal date older than the event that deposited it, short-lived single-entity samples such as carbonised seeds reduce the risk of dating residual material. The move to such short-lived material came first at this site; the resubmission of the same context to another facility followed. The old-wood problem concerns what a charcoal fragment actually dates, whereas the check performed by a second facility on an already-dated context speaks to error in the measurement itself.\n\nReplicate dating of the same context in a second independent laboratory tests for measurement error. At the modelling stage, outlier models in Bayesian chronologies downweight individual dates that conflict with the stratigraphic sequence. The inversion between Layers 3 and 4 therefore caused the discordant determination to be downweighted in the final model, and it is this combination \u2014 a stratigraphically controlled sequence, samples chosen to date the depositional event itself, and independent replication \u2014 rather than any single determination that settles the matter.",
